<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext HTTP is permitted on purpose.

  Since Android 9 the platform blocks http:// by default, which is the right
  default for an app talking to one known service over the internet. This app
  talks to a server the user hosts themselves, and the realistic deployment is
  uvicorn or docker compose on a machine in the same building, reached by LAN
  address. Demanding HTTPS there would mean a certificate for an IP address on a
  private network - which public CAs do not issue - so the practical effect
  would not be "encrypted", it would be "the app does not work".

  What crosses the wire to that server is NOT harmless, and this comment once
  said it was ("no credentials, no personal data, nothing that identifies a
  pupil"). The diary's password is not among it: the app never sends it to our
  server. It goes only to the diary's own origin, taken from the catalog bundled
  with the app, https-only - an origin that is not is dropped from the
  allow-list - and enforced on every request by OriginGuard, with no redirect
  followed. So the cleartext permitted here never carries it: the allow-list
  holds https origins only, and there is no http:// address the sign-in may
  send to.

  What does cross a plain http:// server, readable by anyone on the same school
  Wi-Fi, is:
    - the diary session the diary hands back, which the phone registers with
      our server at POST /api/v1/diary/session - a working credential for the
      child's diary until it expires;
    - the diary bearer that opens the child's marks and attendance through us;
    - the class bearer, which is frequently a WRITE token since personal
      connect codes exist.

  It is still permitted, because the premise above is real: demanding HTTPS on
  a LAN address would mean "the app does not work" rather than "encrypted".
  What changed is that the app says so where it matters: the diary sign-in form
  warns before anything is typed when the server address is not https://
  (diary_insecure_server), and the warning is about the session, not the
  password.

  A school that does terminate TLS simply enters an https:// address and this
  configuration stops applying to it; nothing here downgrades a secure URL.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <!-- System CAs only. User-installed CAs stay untrusted, so adding a
                 certificate to the device cannot silently intercept an https://
                 server the school did set up properly. -->
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
